You can stop the user from accessing array elements beyond the fourth index by checking the index value before accessing it. Here's an example:

```
int[] myArray = new int[5]; // initialize an array with 5 elements

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) // loop 10 times, but the array only has 5 elements
{
    if (i < myArray.Length) // check if the index is within the array bounds
    {
        myArray[i] = i * 2; // assign a value to the array element at index i
    }
    else
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Index out of bounds! Only up to 4 allowed."); // print an error message if index is out of bounds
        break; // stop the loop if index is out of bounds
    }
}
```

In this example, we use a `for` loop to iterate 10 times, but the array `myArray` only has 5 elements. Before assigning a value to an array element, we check if the index `i` is within the bounds of the array using the `myArray.Length` property. If the index is within bounds, we assign a value to the array element. Otherwise, we print an error message and break out of the loop to stop the user from accessing array elements beyond the fourth index.

