```
#include <iostream>
#include <map>

int main() {
    std::map<int, std::string> map1 = {{1, "one"}, {2, "two"}};
    std::map<int, std::string> map2 = {{3, "three"}, {4, "four"}};

    std::map<int, std::string> merged;

    merged.insert(map1.begin(), map1.end());
    merged.insert(map2.begin(), map2.end());

    std::cout << "Merged map: " << std::endl;
    for (const auto& pair : merged) {
        std::cout << pair.first << " : " << pair.second << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we have two maps `map1` and `map2`. We create a third map `merged` to hold the result of merging `map1` and `map2`.

We use the `insert()` function to insert all the key-value pairs from `map1` and `map2` into `merged`. Since `map` stores its key-value pairs in a sorted order, the resulting `merged` map will also be sorted.

Finally, we iterate over the `merged` map and output the key-value pairs one by one.

